(Regression: a buffer reports `Buffer (static)` from its `type` property
however it was declared — bug #42. Every declaration spelling routes
through `Statement::BufferDecl`, which set `variable_types` but never
`declared_types`, so `emit_type_property` missed its lookup and fell
through to the runtime-tag dispatch; the buffer pointer read as a
string tag and printed `Text (dynamic)`. Only the initializer form
`is "seed"` took the `VarDecl` path, which already inserted, so it was
already correct and is kept here as a control. A `value` holding text
must stay `Text (dynamic)` — the whole point of `value` is that its
type is whatever its runtime tag currently holds.)

a number called n is 3.
a buffer called buf1 is 16 bytes in size.
a buffer called buf2 is "seed".
Create a buffer called buf3 with size 16.
a buffer called buf4 is 16 bytes.
a buffer called buf5.
a value called v is "hello".

print n's type.
print buf1's type.
print buf2's type.
print buf3's type.
print buf4's type.
print buf5's type.
print v's type.